<div id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Which parts of procedural moral reasoning are frontier models lacking?</span> Similar as our analysis on thinking traces, we observed that models’ final responses do well (85.1% average) in
avoiding harmful outcomes within their final responses and worst in logical process of moral reasoning (64.9% average). Models perform better in providing helpful outcome in their final responses when compared with their thinking traces. One possible reason is models are better at following explicit instructions in their final response as opposed to in their thinking traces.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
